# Intervals named by counting letters, played on the glockenspiel, and found at the start of tunes a
# child knows, in the observatory where the band plays Twinkle Twinkle under the stars.
lesson music-intervals v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=4 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Intervals, named and heard"
  goal "Name the interval between two notes by counting their letter names, both notes included."

  look {
    scene 30x23 {
      row top space=2 align=end at=canvas(1, 1) {
        observatory dome open=1
        telescope scope tilt=40
      }
      notes tune clef=treble notes=[C4, C4, G4, G4, A4, A4, G4] values=[1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2] meter=4 letters=true below=top gap=1
    }
    say """
      At the observatory's open night the band plays Twinkle Twinkle Little Star. The tune jumps
      from C up to G. To name that jump, count the letter names from the lower note to the higher
      one and count both: C, D, E, F, G is five letters, so the jump is a 5th. On the staff a 5th
      goes from a line to the line two above it. A 2nd is next door and an 8th, the octave, is the
      same letter again. The number counts letters, not size: C up to E and D up to F are both 3rds,
      but C to E is four semitones, a major 3rd, and D to F only three, a minor 3rd, the same gaps
      as in major and minor chords.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice music.interval-count count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    level easy {
      worked music.interval-tune-opening t=0 level=medium
    }
    practice music.interval-play-glock count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice music.interval-tune-opening count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=7 hard-seed=1
    show music.interval-quality k=4 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    practice music.interval-sky-tune count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=2
    show music.interval-play-glock k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show music.interval-tune-opening t=1 easy-t=2 hard-t=3
    level hard {
      show music.interval-claim q=1 r=1
    }
    show music.interval-sky-tune s=2 easy-s=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.interval-claim q=0 r=0 easy-r=1 hard-r=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.interval-all-pairs v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }
  remember {
    say "Count the letters from one note to the other, both included: C up to G is a 5th."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that an interval is named by the number of letter names it spans, counting both
    ends, so C to E is a 3rd and C to G a 5th, and that the same count works on the staff, the
    glockenspiel and the keyboard. The wrong idea to watch for, above all, is counting the gaps,
    which gives an answer one short; the second is losing count past G, where the letters start
    again at A. In the working, look for the child saying the letters aloud, with the first note as
    one. The two-star question is about that gap count, and at hard about two 2nds of different
    sizes; one core question asks whether a 3rd is major or minor, from its semitones, which joins
    this lesson to major and minor chords; the three-star question counts every pair of chime bars
    an interval apart and finds that the count drops by one as the interval grows. A child who is
    stuck can sing up from the lower note, one letter to each step, and go back to the way in; stay
    on this lesson another day if the answers still come out one short. Hearing the jumps is on the
    music page, and on paper the drawing does the work. Off the page, hum the start of Twinkle
    Twinkle and Here Comes the Bride and say which jump is bigger.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the intervals to count are 2nds, 3rds and 4ths, the first jump of Kumbaya is worked once in the teacher's pen, the bars to play are a 3rd or a 4th apart, the 3rd to size is C up to E, a major 3rd, the tunes to read are Here Comes the Bride and Twinkle Twinkle, whose first jump comes after a repeated note, the star tunes use smaller moves, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the three-star counts the pairs a 5th apart."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the intervals to count run from a 5th to the octave, the bars to play are a 4th to an octave apart, the 3rd to size is F sharp up to A, where the sharp takes a semitone away, the tunes are Twinkle Twinkle and My Bonnie, a 6th, the star tunes move down as well as up and past G, a claim about two 2nds of different sizes is judged before the two-star, and the three-star counts the pairs more than a 5th apart."
  }
}
